status":null,"result":"I'm approving PKT-0068. The code change does what the goal asks, keeps everything the contract protects, and is well within budget.\n\n- **The change:** in `DocService/sources/converterservice.js`, synchronous conversions now check for the result after 50 ms and double the wait each time, up to `CONVERT_ASYNC_DELAY` (1000 ms). The timeout check now adds up the time actually waited and still uses the strict `>` test, so `CONVERT_TIMEOUT` triggers by the same rule as before. Lines 120–200 are the only place the function's internals changed. The HTTP request and response shape are untouched.\n- **The tests:** only the expected wait sequences in `tests/unit/converterService.tests.js` changed, plus the matching test title and comment, which is what the contract allows. I checked the numbers by hand:\n  - **Timeout case:** waits of 50, 100, 200, 400, 800, 1000 and 1000 ms add up to 3550 ms, the first total above the 3000 ms timeout, so the expected sequence is right.\n  - **The \"async\" test now expecting `[50]`:** this is correct. That test sets `DEFAULT_SHARD_KEY`, which makes the server fall back to synchronous mode (around lines 413–418), so it does go through the sync loop.\n- **Size:** 5 files and about 96 lines changed, under the budget of 6 files and 800 lines.\n- **Latency measurement:** done on the native stack. Sync docx→odt went from a median of about 1018 ms to about 166 ms, and the method is recorded in the Executor log.\n- **Follow-up request:** `.theseus/spawn/PKT-0068-make-e2e-convert-js-work-against-the-native-jwt.md` is present, so the request is listed even though the \"Spawned packets\" section in the packet is empty (the factory fills that in).\n- **The earlier unit-test failure:** I agree with the executor that it was a flaky test. It was a 5 s startup timeout in `tests/unit/request.tests.js`, which is outside this packet's target and has nothing to do with `converterservice.js`.\n- **Logs:** the Executor log and the friction line are both there.\n\nThere is one tradeoff, and it's acceptable. Each sync request now makes up to about 6 extra database queries in its first 1.5 s.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVE backoff polling keeps the timeout rule, only the wait expectations changed in the tests, latency measured (~1018→166 ms), follow-up recorded in spawn/","ttft_ms":2576,"type":"result","duration_ms":15500,"uuid":"327fc58c-0a54-4df7-a2c0-ce4b82aa2f44","ttft_stream_ms":1284,"time_to_request_ms":444,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":11,"system_prompt":171,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":5,"input_attachments":26,"input_hooks":3,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":86,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":10,"init_frame_send":4,"engine_pickup":2,"loop_entry":4,"message_prep":2,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":3,"request_announcements":16,"query_setup":5,"auto_mode_context_wait":47,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":31,"message_normalization":3,"client_creation":3,"request_build":10},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":100,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":275,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"process_rss_mib_turn_start":214,"process_anon_mib_turn_start":139,"process_rss_mib_result":224,"process_anon_mib_result":136,"process_peak_rss_mib":239,"process_cpu_ms_turn":1036,"process_wall_ms_turn":15499,"process_cpu_ms_streaming":359,"process_wall_ms_streaming":12152,"main_stream_count":4,"main_stream_event_count":238,"main_stream_chunk_count":63,"input_attachments_detail":{"slowest_producer":"artifact_opening_prefetch","slowest_producer_ms":9,"cpu_ms":22,"major_faults":0,"setup_ms":1,"prompt_producers_ms":0,"ambient_start_ms":10,"ambient_wait_ms":9,"render_ms":2,"producers":41,"token_counts":0},"first_content_frame_ms":1284,"time_to_request_from_spawn_ms":21266761,"warm_spare_claimed":false,"flag_fetch_kick":"started_in_init_warm","time_origin_ms":1793656723043.3716,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"process_start_tuning":0}
